\begin{abstract}
We measure how knowledge-edit effects behave under real post-training INT8 and NF4
quantization. Across three models (Llama-3.2-1B L12, Llama-3.2-3B L24,
Qwen-2.5-1.5B L21), three editors (ROME, MEMIT, AlphaEdit), three seeds, and 27
model--editor--seed cells, we apply the bitsandbytes INT8 vectorwise and NF4
double-quant codec kernels to the edited MLP and to the full model. ROME efficacy
exceeds the preregistered 0.80 NF4 threshold on the primary Llama-3.2-1B and
Qwen-2.5-1.5B cells. The stronger prediction that MEMIT and AlphaEdit are no less
robust than ROME holds on every Llama-1B arm but fails on the Qwen NF4 edited-layer
and full-model arms; AlphaEdit also trails ROME marginally under Qwen INT8
full-model. Qwen absolute edit-success rates in FP32 are \pQwenRomeFpThirtyTwoAbsEsrPt{} (ROME), \pQwenMemitFpThirtyTwoAbsEsrPt{}
(MEMIT), \pQwenAlphaFpThirtyTwoAbsEsrPt{} (AlphaEdit); under NF4 full-model they fall to \pQwenRomeNfFourFMAbsEsrPt{}, \pQwenMemitNfFourFMAbsEsrPt{}, \pQwenAlphaNfFourFMAbsEsrPt{}
respectively, and the conditional survival given FP32 succeeded is
\pGridQOneRomeNfFourFMCond{} (ROME), \pGridQOneMemitNfFourFMCond{} (MEMIT),
and \pGridQOneAlphaNfFourFMCond{} (AlphaEdit). Damage-rank survival is preserved under INT8 and edited-layer NF4 on both
validated-law ROME cells. Under full-model NF4, the legacy flattened-grid
rank passes at Llama-3.2-1B L12 (0.904) but fails at Llama-3.2-3B L24
(0.680); the v1.2.1 repair artefact distinguishes this from the operational
edit-level signed-mean rank, which remains near-unity on both validated-law
ROME cells (Llama-1B 0.997 [0.996,0.998]; Llama-3B 0.997 [0.988,0.998]).
The amended channel-scale $\times$ NF4 minimum-gap K3 axis remains
unadjudicated pending a targeted rerun; the legacy/local-gap sensitivity values
that the readout reports are not used for directionality. INT8 has no
outlier-decomposed bin-width axis (threshold = 0 in this paper's accounting), so
its F\_above and median-ratio columns are reported only for completeness and are
not adjudicated against the amended K3 axis. A descriptive reconstruction-gap
signature ($r_{\mathrm{func}} < r_{\mathrm{param}}$ in every C3 scheme block; median
$r_{\mathrm{func}}/r_{\mathrm{param}}$ 0.00570 at INT8 and 0.00591 at NF4) is
reported as a summary of the total-rounding error budget; the epsilon is
implemented as $\mathrm{Q}(W{+}d)-(W{+}d)$, so the statistic is a gap, not a
quantized-update error, and the 18/18 ordering is descriptive evidence, not a
unique mechanism. Generation checks at $n = 40$ prompts show mean perplexity rises
of \pPplRisePctMin{}--\pPplRisePctMax{}\% from FP32-edited to NF4 full-model across cells; paraphrase ESR is
unchanged within noise at the edited-layer arms and mildly attenuated at full
model. The preregistration document was a DRAFT (not ratified as active) and is
included for the record; the core thresholds (C1, C2, K1/K2/K3) were specified
prospectively in the design document; the prereg carries dated
protocol-level entries on the bin-width denominator and the F1 definition whose
chronological status (pre- vs.\ post-data) is not adjudicated here, since file
mtimes of the preregistration document fall after several real cells and the
document remained a DRAFT throughout. The Llama-3.1-8B
cell from the prereg grid was omitted from this submission (its FP32 within-probe
law is dead at the tested layer, so it would not have gated any claim). The
40-edit generation check protocol was executed as part of Track 1 and
its numbers are reported in Section~\ref{sec:c12}; the deployed-kernel
(Track 2, GPTQ/AWQ) bridge measurement is out of scope of this submission.
