\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Edited language models are deployed quantized. INT8 weight quantization and 4-bit
double-quant are standard cost-control choices for serving
LLMs~\cite{hallucinationsurvey2023,kesurvey2023}, and rank-one knowledge edits
(ROME~\cite{rome2022}, MEMIT~\cite{memit2023}, AlphaEdit~\cite{alphaedit2025}) are
increasingly used to keep a deployed model's facts current without retraining. The
combination---edit then quantize---is therefore the realistic deployment path, and yet
editing and compression are usually evaluated in isolation. Where they are studied
together, the work focuses on absolute Strict-Edit-Locality F1 and reports that
quantization degrades locality; we do not replicate the F1 measurement here, because
the bridge cell that would let us compare F1 and our relative ranking on the same
edits is the deployed-kernel driver, which is PENDING (Section~\ref{sec:bridge}).

This paper reframes the question. A deployer deciding which edits to keep does not care
about an absolute F1 number in isolation; the deployer cares about the \emph{rank order}
of edits by collateral damage, because the order determines which edits to admit into
the next release. We therefore measure efficacy survival (conditional on FP32 succeeding
and absolute across the full edit set) and \emph{multilevel} damage-rank survival on
the same edits installed before and after quantization. The damage metric is the
signed logit difference on held-out probes disjoint from the edit set---never AUROC,
which is a probe-marginal artifact in this setting. The multilevel distinction
discriminates (i) the legacy \emph{flattened-grid} rank
$\operatorname{Spearman}(D^{\mathrm{FP32}}.\mathrm{ravel}(),
D^{q}.\mathrm{ravel}())$, (ii) a \emph{within-probe} rank averaged across
non-degenerate probe columns, and (iii) the operational \emph{edit-level
signed-mean} rank, the per-edit Spearman of $\overline{D}^{\mathrm{FP32}}$ vs
$\overline{D}^{q}$ across edits---together with the magnitude-sensitivity
statistics \emph{edit-level absmean} and \emph{edit-level L2}, and the
\emph{base-quant-noise mean $|D|$} under the UNEDITED-quantized baseline
arm. The edit-level signed-mean is the closest operational proxy for the
deployer's question because it pools damage to the per-edit level before
taking Spearman.

\textbf{Codec round-trip vs.\ packed deployment inference.} This paper's Track-1
measurement is the codec round-trip: install the edit in FP32, run
bitsandbytes \texttt{BitsAndBytesWeightQuantizer.quantize}/\texttt{dequantize}
(or the linear-modules \texttt{Linear8bitLt}/4-bit wrappers) on the edited MLP
or on every block linear, and re-measure against the same edit in FP32. This is
\emph{not} the packed-deployment-inference protocol of model-level quantization
followed by forward pass on the packed weights; that protocol is the deployed-kernel
Track-2 bridge, which is PENDING. The codec round-trip isolates the quantization
kernel's effect on the edit; the packed-deployment protocol adds the inference-stack
cost on top of it. We report the codec-round-trip numbers in this submission.

The second question is \emph{why}. A natural hypothesis, ported from the
unlearning$\times$quantization literature, is that concentrated parametric edits
survive quantization because their per-parameter changes $|\Delta W|/b$ sit at or above
the quantization bin width, while diffuse gradient-style updates sit far below it. We
test this hypothesis with a per-cell, per-scheme bin-width statistic, but the existing
NPZ readout uses a legacy local-gap sensitivity denominator rather than the
preregistered amended channel-scale $\times$ NF4 minimum-gap denominator. The amended
primary axis is therefore unadjudicated pending a targeted rerun. The legacy readout
has median $|\Delta W|/b \approx 0.03$ on the validated ROME cells. A descriptive
reconstruction-gap signature, $r_{\mathrm{func}} < r_{\mathrm{param}}$ in every
evaluated block, is reported as a summary of the total-rounding error budget; it is
not a unique mechanism, and the epsilon implementation
$\mathrm{Q}(W{+}d)-(W{+}d)$ makes it a total-rounding gap rather than a
quantized-update error.

\textbf{Preregistration disclosure.} The preregistration document for this study
was prepared as a DRAFT and was \emph{not} ratified as active before any Track-1
Phase-1 cell ran; the cell runs proceeded under the project's house
pre-specification convention. The DRAFT is included in the supplementary record
for transparency. Core thresholds (C1 efficacy survival $\geq 0.90$ at 8-bit,
$\geq 0.80$ at 4-bit; C2 $|\Delta\rho| \leq 0.15$ and rank survival $\geq 0.95$
at 8-bit/edited-layer, $\geq 0.85$ at 4-bit full-model; K1/K2/K3) were specified
prospectively in the design document and reproduced in the DRAFT. The DRAFT
carries two dated protocol-level entries: (i) the bin-width denominator for K3
is recorded as a channel-scale $\times$ NF4 minimum-gap convention aligned with
the donor source, and (ii) the Strict-Edit-Locality F1 definition is recorded
as a mirror of the donor's reference. The chronological ordering of these
entries relative to the cell runs is not adjudicated here (file mtimes of the
preregistration document fall after several real cells, and the DRAFT was
never ratified as active); the manuscript reports the entries without a
