\section{Claims and specified design}
\label{sec:claims}

We evaluate four directional claims, frozen before any Track-1 Phase-1 run, against the
nine-cell aggregate readout. The specified thresholds are listed in
Table~\ref{tab:track1}; gate outcomes are listed in Table~\ref{tab:gates}.

\textbf{C1 (efficacy survival).} ROME retains edit success with conditional probability
$\geq$\pEsrSurvEightThreshold{} at 8-bit and $\geq$\pEsrSurvFourThreshold{} at 4-bit on
the preregistered primary models Llama-3.2-1B and Qwen-2.5-1.5B. The stronger
prediction that MEMIT and AlphaEdit are no less robust than ROME is evaluated
separately. \textbf{Outcome}: threshold survival holds for all three editors, but the
no-less-robust ordering is architecture-dependent. It holds on every Llama-1B arm,
but fails for both alternative editors on Qwen NF4 edited-layer and full-model; it
also fails marginally for AlphaEdit on Qwen INT8 full-model. The Qwen full-model NF4
conditional-survival values are \pGridQOneRomeNfFourFMCond{} (ROME),
\pGridQOneMemitNfFourFMCond{} (MEMIT), and
\pGridQOneAlphaNfFourFMCond{} (AlphaEdit).
The Qwen absolute edit-success rates are \pQwenRomeFpThirtyTwoAbsEsrPt{} (ROME), \pQwenMemitFpThirtyTwoAbsEsrPt{} (MEMIT), \pQwenAlphaFpThirtyTwoAbsEsrPt{}
(AlphaEdit) in FP32; under NF4 full-model they fall to 0.405, 0.410, 0.385
respectively. Llama-3.2-3B is the mandatory C2 extension and is reported separately.

\textbf{C2 (geometric damage-ranking survival).} \textit{Where the FP32 law validates}
($\rho_{\mathrm{fp32}}^{\mathrm{within}} \geq$\pFpThreshold{}, established in-cell),
the Spearman between key-cosine and signed damage stays within $\pm$\pDeltaRhoTolerance{}
of the FP32 value. Damage-rank survival must be $\geq$\pRankSurvEightThreshold{} under
INT8 and edited-layer quantization and $\geq$\pRankSurvFourThreshold{} under 4-bit
full-model quantization. \textbf{Scope narrowed by data}: both validated ROME cells
preserve the ranking under INT8 and edited-layer NF4. Under full-model NF4,
Llama-3.2-1B L12 passes (rank survival \pPrimarynfFourdqFullModelRankSurv{}) while
Llama-3.2-3B L24 fails (\pSecondnfFourdqFullModelRankSurv{} against threshold
\pRankSurvFourThreshold{}). MEMIT and AlphaEdit are excluded by C2's preregistered
ROME-only scope; Qwen-2.5-1.5B is a degeneracy datapoint, not a C2 cell.

\textbf{Rank-survival definition.} The C2 number reported in
Table~\ref{tab:track1} is the \emph{flattened-grid rank}, computed as
$\operatorname{Spearman}(D^{\mathrm{FP32}}.\mathrm{ravel}(),
D^{q}.\mathrm{ravel}())$ over the held-out probe set for the cells pooled across
seeds; we mark it as the legacy sensitivity statistic. The v1.2.1 repair
artefact (\texttt{quant\_survival\_repair\_v1.json}, n\_boot = \pRepairNBoot{},
hierarchical bootstrap seeds-then-edits) supplies three further per-arm
multilevel rank-survival statistics: a \emph{within-probe} rank (cross-edit
Spearman averaged within each non-degenerate probe column), an \emph{edit-level
signed-mean} rank (per-edit Spearman of $\overline{D}^{\mathrm{FP32}}$ vs
$\overline{D}^{q}$ across edits), and magnitude-sensitivity statistics
(\emph{edit-level absmean}, \emph{edit-level L2}, and the
\emph{base-quant-noise mean $|D|$} under the UNEDITED-quantized baseline arm).
The flattened-grid rank, within-probe rank, and edit-level signed-mean rank
together let us distinguish the regime on which the ranking is preserved; the
edit-level signed-mean is the closest operational proxy for the deployer's
question because it pools damage to the per-edit level before taking Spearman.
The repair artefact is the source for every operational number cited in this
paper.

\textbf{C3 (bin-width mechanism).} M-concentration is evaluated on the amended
channel-scale $\times$ NF4 minimum-gap axis. The existing NPZ files contain only the
legacy/local-gap sensitivity axis, with
$b=\operatorname{absmax}_{\mathrm{block}}\,\operatorname{local\_gap}(w/\operatorname{absmax}_{\mathrm{block}})$
(blocksize 64). The amended primary axis is unadjudicated pending a targeted rerun.
The 18/18 reconstruction-gap ordering ($r_{\mathrm{func}}<r_{\mathrm{param}}$) is
retained as descriptive evidence, not as a substitute for K3 adjudication and not
as a unique mechanism. INT8 has no outlier-decomposed bin-width axis in this
paper's accounting (threshold = 0): the kernel is vectorwise with an outlier
dimension, so the bin-width quantity is nominal, and the F\_above/median-ratio
columns for INT8 in Table~\ref{tab:c3} are reported for completeness without an
adjudication against the amended K3 axis.

\textbf{C4 (reconciliation with absolute locality).} In the bridge cell (MEMIT +
GPTQ-4bit) we will measure Hase et~al.'s Strict-Edit-Locality F1 alongside our signed
damage rank on the same edits; the expectation is that F1 degrades while rank survives.
\textbf{C4 is PENDING}: the deployed-kernel Track-2 driver is not part of this
submission; the bridge cell will run only after user ratification of the Track-2
protocol. We do not claim a measured replication.

