The reconstruction-gap descriptive statistic is the ratio of the
\emph{total-rounding gap} $r_{\mathrm{func}}=\|\epsilon x\|/\|\Delta W x\|$ on
the edit's key activation $x$ to the per-parameter relative error
$r_{\mathrm{param}}=\operatorname{median}|\epsilon|/\operatorname{median}|\Delta W|$,
where $\epsilon = \mathrm{Q}(W{+}d) - (W{+}d)$. Because the epsilon is implemented
as the gap between the quantized edited weight and the unquantized edited weight
(not as $\Delta W_q - \Delta W$), it is a total-rounding gap rather than a
quantized-update error; the 18/18 reconstruction-gap ordering
($r_{\mathrm{func}} < r_{\mathrm{param}}$ in every C3 scheme block of the
readout, nine model--editor cells per scheme) is therefore descriptive evidence
of the gap, not a unique mechanism. Across the readout the median
$r_{\mathrm{func}}/r_{\mathrm{param}}$ is $0.00570$ at INT8 and $0.00591$ at NF4.

On Llama-3.2-1B L12 ROME NF4 specifically,
$r_{\mathrm{func}} = \pPrimaryNfFourDqRfunc{}$ and
$r_{\mathrm{param}} = \pPrimaryNfFourDqRparam{}$, a reconstruction-to-parameter
gap ratio below 1\% on the legacy readout. These values summarize the
total-rounding error budget; they do not establish the amended concentration
verdict, do not adjudicate the amended K3 axis, and do not by themselves imply
a unique causal mechanism.

